For a countdown you start high and *decrease* the variable each pass. I'll use `-= 1` and loop while `count > 0`:

```jac
with entry {
    count = 10;
    while count > 0 {
        print(count);
        count -= 1;
    }
    print("Blast off!");
}
```

This prints `10, 9, 8, ... 1`, and once `count` hits 0 the condition `count > 0` is false so the loop ends and it prints `Blast off!`.

Not quite — with `count >= 0` the loop would also run when `count` is 0, so it would print an extra `0` before `Blast off!`. Since you want the countdown to end at 1, `count > 0` is the correct condition:

```jac
with entry {
    count = 10;
    while count > 0 {
        print(count);
        count -= 1;
    }
    print("Blast off!");
}
```

That prints `10` through `1`, then `Blast off!` — no stray `0`.

