Split the `Greeter` walker into declaration + impl. The abilities have odd names (`greet` on Root, `say_hello` on Person) -- make sure the impl targets match. Code:

```jac
node Person { has name: str; has age: int; }
edge Knows { has since: int; }

walker Greeter {
    can greet with Root entry { visit [-->]; }
    can say_hello with Person entry {
        print(f"Hello, {here.name}!");
        visit [-->];
    }
}

with entry {
    alice = Person(name="Alice", age=30);
    bob = Person(name="Bob", age=25);
    root ++> alice;
    alice +>: Knows(since=2020) :+> bob;
    root spawn Greeter();
}
```

The impl name is the ability name, independent of which node type it triggers on -- so it's `impl Greeter.greet` (the Root-entry one) and `impl Greeter.say_hello` (the Person-entry one). The `with X entry` clause stays on the declaration. Here:

```jac
node Person {
    has name: str;
    has age: int;
}
edge Knows {
    has since: int;
}
walker Greeter {
    can greet with Root entry;
    can say_hello with Person entry;
}
impl Greeter.greet {
    visit [-->];
}
impl Greeter.say_hello {
    print(f"Hello, {here.name}!");
    visit [-->];
}
with entry {
    alice = Person(name="Alice", age=30);
    bob = Person(name="Bob", age=25);
    root ++> alice;
    alice +>: Knows(since=2020) :+> bob;
    root spawn Greeter();
}
```

Exactly right. The trigger clause (`with Root entry`, `with Person entry`) lives on the declaration inside the walker; the `impl` block carries only the body and must not repeat the `with ...` part. Matching is by name: `impl Greeter.greet` pairs with `can greet with Root entry;`. Traversal is unchanged -- `root spawn Greeter()` prints `Hello, Alice!` then `Hello, Bob!`.

