Only the `disengage`. A plain `return;` is fine inside an `impl` (it's ordinary control flow). If `explore` had just the `return;` and no `disengage`, I could have externalized it too. It's specifically `disengage` (walker-lifecycle control) that the compiler pins to an inline ability body. The version above is correct -- `cs301 spawn CanTake(...)` still prints `You can take CS301!`.

