Split this lexer heuristic so the declaration and body are separate (`def` signature up top, `impl` below):

```jac
import from pygments.util { shebang_matches }

def analyse_text(text: str) -> bool {
    return shebang_matches(text, r"pythonw?(3(\.\d)?)?") or "import " in text[:1000];
}
```

Straightforward split:

```jac
import from pygments.util { shebang_matches }

def analyse_text(text: str) -> bool;

impl analyse_text {
    return shebang_matches(text, r"pythonw?(3(\.\d)?)?") or "import " in text[:1000];
}
```

It's not the split itself — it's that once there's an explicit `-> bool` on the declaration, the checker enforces the return type strictly. `shebang_matches` comes from an untyped Python module, so it's inferred as `object`, and `object or bool` widens the whole expression to `object | bool`, which isn't `bool`. Wrap the untyped call in `bool(...)` to pin it:

```jac
"""analyse_text: declaration separated from an impl body (bool-cast keeps the return type)."""

import from pygments.util { shebang_matches }

def analyse_text(text: str) -> bool;

impl analyse_text {
    return bool(shebang_matches(text, r"pythonw?(3(\.\d)?)?")) or "import " in text[:1000];
}

with entry {
    print(analyse_text("import os;"));
    print(analyse_text("#!/usr/bin/env python3\nx = 1;"));
    print(analyse_text("walker foo {}"));
}
