This is the classic manual-traversal OOP style with an explicit connections list. I want to rewrite it the idiomatic Jac way -- real `node`/`edge` and a `walker` instead of the `obj` + `def find_friends` loop. Same result (`Found 1 friend(s)`). Here's the code:

```jac
obj Person {
    has name: str;
    has connections: list = [];
}

obj Connection {
    has conn_type: str;
    has target: object;
}

def find_friends(person: Person) -> list {
    friends = [];
    for connection in person.connections {
        if connection.conn_type == "friend" {
            friends.append(connection.target);
        }
    }
    return friends;
}

with entry {
    alice = Person(name="Alice");
    bob = Person(name="Bob");

    conn = Connection(conn_type="friend", target=bob);
    alice.connections.append(conn);

    friends = find_friends(alice);
    print(f"Found {len(friends)} friend(s)");
}
```

Right -- in OSP the `Connection` obj becomes an actual `edge`, and `find_friends` becomes a walker that traverses friend edges instead of iterating a list. First cut:

```jac
node Person { has name: str; }
edge Friend {}

walker FindFriends {
    has friends: list = [];
    can collect with Person entry {
        for f in [here ->:Friend:->] { self.friends.append(f); }
    }
}

with entry {
    alice = Person(name="Alice");
    bob = Person(name="Bob");
    alice +>:Friend:+> bob;
    w = FindFriends();
    alice spawn w;
    print(f"Found {len(w.friends)} friend(s)");
}
```

Two good catches. The `conn_type` filter is preserved structurally: instead of storing a string and checking it, the relationship *is* a typed `Friend` edge, so `[here ->:Friend:->]` only ever returns friends -- that's the OSP way to encode "conn_type == friend". On anchoring: it does run as-is because I spawn on `alice` directly and `collect` fires on `here`, but you're right that the conventional pattern is to hang nodes off `root` and start there. Let me do it that way so it matches how you'd normally structure it -- `root ++> alice`, spawn on `root`, a `start` ability visits out to the people:

```jac
node Person {
    has name: str;
}
edge Friend {}
walker FindFriends {
    has friends: list = [];
    can start with Root entry;
    can collect with Person entry;
}
impl FindFriends.start {
    visit [-->];
}
impl FindFriends.collect {
    for f in [here ->:Friend:->] {
        self.friends.append(f);
    }
}
with entry {
    alice = Person(name="Alice");
    bob = Person(name="Bob");
    root ++> alice;
    alice +>:Friend:+> bob;
    w = FindFriends();
    root spawn w;
    print(f"Found {len(w.friends)} friend(s)");
}
```

Sure. The idiomatic way is a walker with an ability that fires on each node type and a counter field it bumps as it goes. Here's a first cut that counts only the data nodes:

```jac
node DataNode {
    has value: int;
}

walker Counter {
    has count: int = 0;

    can count_nodes with DataNode entry {
        self.count += 1;
        visit [-->];
    }
}

with entry {
    root ++> DataNode(value=1) ++> DataNode(value=2);
    walker_instance = Counter();
    root spawn walker_instance;
    print(f"Counted {walker_instance.count} nodes");
}
```

That visits the two DataNodes and prints 2.

Got it -- then we add a second ability that fires on `Root entry` and bumps the counter there too before it descends. The root ability increments once, then each DataNode ability increments as the walker visits down the chain:

```jac
node DataNode {
    has value: int;
}

walker Counter {
    has count: int = 0;

    can start with Root entry {
        self.count += 1;
        visit [-->];
    }

    can count_nodes with DataNode entry {
        self.count += 1;
        visit [-->];
    }
}

with entry {
    root ++> DataNode(value=1) ++> DataNode(value=2);
    walker_instance = Counter();
    root spawn walker_instance;
    print(f"Counted {walker_instance.count} nodes");  # Output: 3
}
```

Now the root counts as 1 and the two DataNodes add 2 more, so it prints `Counted 3 nodes`.

