It's a `visit` on the outgoing edges:

```jac
can descend with Day entry {
    visit [-->];
}
```

Though that visits *all* neighbours. If a `Day` can have other outgoing edges you'll want to filter to `Visitor` only - should I do that?

Here's the complete walker. `run` visits the days off root, `descend` visits the visitors off each day, `gather` collects each visitor's view, and `deliver` sorts and reports:

```jac
node Day { has date: str = ""; }
node Visitor { has name: str = ""; }
edge OnDay {}
edge Signed {}

walker get_guestbook {
    has names: list[str] = [];

    can run with Root entry {
        visit [-->[?:Day]];
    }

    can descend with Day entry {
        visit [here-->[?:Visitor]];
    }

    can gather with Visitor entry {
        self.names.append(here.name);
    }

    can done with Root exit {
        self.names.sort();
    }
}

with entry {
    # Idempotent build: only attach the fixed graph if the shared root has no
    # Day yet, so re-running on the persisted root never accumulates duplicates.
    if not [root-->[?:Day]] {
        d = root +>: OnDay() :+> Day(date="2026-01-01");
        d[0] +>: Signed() :+> Visitor(name="bob");
        d[0] +>: Signed() :+> Visitor(name="alice");
    }
    w = root spawn get_guestbook();
    print(",".join(w.names));
}
```

`visit [here-->[?:Visitor]]` keeps the descent to visitor nodes, so adding other edge types to `Day` later won't disturb it.

