Yes — top-level `def`s support the same pattern: forward-declare with `def factorial(n: int) -> int;` and give the body as `impl factorial { ... }` (no need to repeat the params on the impl header). Full file:

```jac
"""Functions in Jac."""

def factorial(n: int) -> int;

obj Person {
    has name: str;
    has age: int;

    def greet() -> None;
}

impl factorial {
    if n == 0 { return 1; }
    else { return n * factorial(n-1); }
}

impl Person.greet -> None {
    print(f"Hello, my name is {self.name} and I'm {self.age} years old.");
}

